right is it rational or is it a fad of  some type there are a lot of fads and  thought  um so hopefully that helps somehow uh  but yes  i think about these things a lot hello  tim this is christopher from  norway about eight years ago i read your  book the four hour work week and i quit  my job  because of it it's the single best  decision i ever made it's led me into  different path  i'm a sound engineer i'm a recording  studio and i'm a  web developer and also a owner of a deli  shop  in a small town in norway and because of  your book  there's a small deli shop located in a  small town in norway  strange my question is uh regarding the  post-production of  your podcast what's the routine like and  do you outsource  the cutting editing the sound the  enhancement  hey man i love the fact that there's a  shop in a small town in norway because  of the book or  more accurately because of what you did  after reading the book so  someday i hope to visit love norway love  love that country  so let's see post production on the  podcast is pretty simple i  record that's usually via zen caster or  squad cast or skype they all have bugs  so i'll just say that in advance none of  them are perfect and then  that will provide audio from both sides  i will record backup audio via quicktime  on the laptop generally using atr 2100  there's a new name  for the newer model that has a usbc  connection so i'm using that  and that's on the production side so now  we have all the files the files go into  dropbox  then that dropbox link will go into  slack there are a few different ways we  do this but i'll go into a slack channel  and the engineer who works on the audio  as well as my producer both in that  channel and  then i will indicate headline options  edits so i'll take notes as we're  recording the podcast  with a guest on things that might need  editing or things that might need  to be removed if especially if they  request things and then  that those files are immediately sent to  transcription  so that the transcript can be put into a  google doc so that if my producer has  any suggested edits  or any questions for me those can all be  added into a google doc those get  approved  and i find those tasks because they're  added to asana by the producer  and that's about  it but sometimes there are things for me  to approve beforehand and those will all  go into asana to get my attention  and then off it goes into the interwebs  hey tim as i'm moving more into a  managerial role  in my full-time work as well as having  very serious hobbies of  jiu-jitsu and cooking i'm having  difficulty striking that balance  of creating more than you consume and i  was just curious to hear if you had any  additional frameworks or strategies or  ways of approaching  the create consume issue  amen so a couple things on creating  and counterbalancing the managing i  would say  with the jiu jitsu there's a lot of  creativity there  certainly you could check out i mean you  would be consuming on one hand  but it would be in the service of  creating anything online by marcelo  garcia's academy  which was co-founded with josh waitzkin  i know they are probably not  doing any classes currently but you can  see back catalog  and it's thematically organized to buy  transition position technique etc  so you can see some amazing stuff  especially in sparring for the  cooking i mean this seems  self-interested but for our chef has a  lot of experimentation that you can add  in  and what's nice about the  experimentation in the four-hour chef is  that  it's intended to teach you principles  and tools you may be already super  advanced if so this might not apply but  it teaches you both kind of the grammar  and the idiom and the vocabulary of  cooking in a way that allows you to then  take those things and translate them  all over the place to different cuisines  so that could be fun to experiment with  the very least check out the egg part  which allows you to learn the flavor  combinations  of different regions and countries  that's pretty easy and pretty fun  so i'll just give that to you as a  starting point hi tim  my question is about your research with  psychedelics  and the narrative around psychedelics  the data will speak for itself on  whether they work  but it seems like with your your  firepower on marketing  it could be uh great for the cause to  put some of that  behind it to help change that narrative  and get the word out  howdy so on the psychedelic front thanks  so much for the kind words i'm very very  excited about certainly the scientific  side and as things scale  as you have the experiences  and certainly administration of these  compounds going from  thousands to tens of thousands to  hundreds of thousands possibly let's say  in any given  year right or even any given month then  there are going to be bad things that  happen that's just the way  that the laws of big numbers work so i  think  what's going to be very very important  is getting insurance on board to both  lower the costs  and also assess risk because that will  be part of  the process for  evaluating anything for insurance  coverage there also needs to be  self-policing and  that that means trade organizations or  just uh  organizations could be non-profit that  help to ensure  that best practices are followed right  so there could be  incentives and public pressure through  organizations that would bring together  say clinics of different types and i'm  sure these exist for ketamine but  across the board this will be more and  more important since if you don't want  regulations  to crack down then you need to  self-police  and you need to have structures in place  and incentives in place  that help ensure people are being their  their best selves organizationally and  personally and as practitioners so  hopefully that makes sense i'm going to  do my best  and thanks for the video hi jim you've  spoken a couple times recently about  the impor