 which you can also use  for swimming i find to conform very  nicely to the side of the head  hey tim my question is about questions  how do you collect your questions and  then from there how do you test your  questions do you  deliberately try them out in  conversation with friends and loved ones  how do they then go from that testing  stage to  okay i'm going to try this in a podcast  hey david yeah i think a lot about  questions i love questions i  typically record questions and i come  across them in conversation or elsewhere  in notes  first natively on the phone and then i  will often  take them and copy them over into  evernote or  increasingly i'll be using rome rome  research i don't really test them out on  friends and family  i will very often just jump into the hot  water with a podcast guest and test it  out because  i rarely record live in front of any  type of audience which means if it  doesn't work  we can just cut it out in  post-production what i've found over  time also  is that it's important to take note of  questions that work  and questions that don't but if you ask  especially a really good  follow-up question they give an answer  what did you learn from that  right or they give you an answer how did  that make you feel what was it like when  a b  c happened right these types of very  simple follow-up questions  can really yield a lot of gold and in  some ways the follow-ups are more  important than the  openers hi tim i'm 25 years old i'm here  in ireland i'm planning on completely  re-skilling  myself and going into different fields  what's your advice on the process of  re-skilling  re-skilling it's not it's not a term i'm  terribly familiar with but i think i do  get the basic idea  i think the best time to explore new  terrain is very often when you are  gainfully employed you could  potentially look at consulting with  one or more startups or working for free  for  one or more startups for a period of  time in addition to your current